{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update13.json\",\"summary\":\"Opened a third investigation in a different mechanism - the Node wrapper's proposal channel - and recorded its current state rather than a claim. The target's own comment at node.go:409-411 calls the proposal-channel reset 'not very sound and likely has bugs', and reading the code shows the channel is closed when an applied change removes the local node (the guard requires it was in Progress before, is not after, and is in neither voter list) while the only re-opening site is the leadership-change branch with a leader known, so a change that adds the node back takes the okBefore=false path and leaves the channel closed. Recorded sources for the guard, the restore branch, the nil-channel select in Node.Propose and Propose's 'proposals can be lost' documentation, plus two behaviours (the close/restore mechanism; Propose blocking on a nil channel until the caller's context or the node's done channel fires). A draft probe (assurance_node_proposal_test.go) drives a wrapped node against a bare leader, removes and re-adds that node through the real propose/apply path, and compares Propose before the removal, after the re-add and after a leadership change; the native run showed the blocked proposal after the re-add (context deadline exceeded while the node was a member again and still knew leader 1, with the leader's index unchanged) but the run is not admissible: the probe read the node's status immediately after delivering a message, and Node.Step only hands the message to the run loop, so the same run's control saw no leader yet and the block cannot be separated from the competing explanation that the channel was closed for lack of a known leader. The interpretation is therefore unresolved with defect_claim unresolved, keeping that counterevidence and the sampling flaw as limitations; the next action is to make the probe wait for the observable member/leader transition before each observation and keep the leadership-change control, after which an execution can be requested.\"}"}}
